# gVisor C/R warm-tier rootfs for box-js, built on top of boxjs-cold-worker:dev. The
# container is CHECKPOINTED with node and the box-js module graph already resident, and
# RESTORED per job.
#
# WARM IS WORTH IT HERE — BUT NOT FOR THE REASON THIS FILE ORIGINALLY GAVE.
#
# The first version of this comment reasoned that warm only avoids node's startup plus
# require()-ing box-js's module graph, guessed that at "a fixed cost in the low hundreds
# of milliseconds", and concluded a cold worker was probably fine. It then said to
# MEASURE before choosing. Measured on toolz2, 3 runs each, boxjs-cold-worker:dev:
#
#   bare node startup                                47 ms
#   node + require box-js                            91 ms   <- what the guess was about
#   full run in a RESIDENT container, trivial sample 2062 ms
#   full run in a RESIDENT container, real sample    1622 ms
#   bare `docker run --rm ... true` (runc)           4216 ms  <- the actual dominant cost
#   the same under runsc                             4496 ms
#
# So the guess was right about its own number and wrong about which number mattered.
# Node startup plus the module graph is 91 ms — about 5% of a job. CONTAINER CREATION is
# ~4.2 s, more than twice the emulation itself, and that is precisely what a warm pool
# removes by keeping the sandbox resident. Warm turns a ~6 s cold job into a ~2 s one.
#
# Two further things the measurement says, both worth keeping:
#
#   * Emulation is NOT the bottleneck, and sample complexity barely moves it — a trivial
#     two-line sample took LONGER (2062 ms) than a 13 KB obfuscated one (1622 ms). Per-run
#     cost is dominated by box-js's fixed pipeline (rewrite, timeouts), not by the script.
#     Do not tune this tier by making samples simpler.
#   * runsc costs ~280 ms more per container than runc at startup. Irrelevant warm, since
#     it is paid once per resident sandbox rather than once per sample.
#
# NO READINESS ENTRYPOINT. box-js has no daemon and no separate readiness state: node
# starts, requires box-js, and is immediately able to run. The cold image already proved
# at build time that the emulator executes. A restore that somehow lacked a working
# node_modules would fail the job loudly with no_results_dir rather than silently
# reporting every sample clean — which is exactly why the ClamAV rootfs needs a readiness
# gate and this one does not.
#
# Build context = this repo's root:
#   docker build --build-arg BASE=boxjs-cold-worker:dev \
#     -f deploy/gvisor/Dockerfile.boxjs -t boxjs-warm:gvisor .
ARG BASE=boxjs-cold-worker:dev
FROM ${BASE}
ARG ENGINE=boxjs_engine.engine:BoxJsEngine
USER root

# run_warm.py and engines.py come from the blastbox checkout; BLASTBOX_SRC points at it.
# They are not vendored here — pinning framework files in an engine repo is how a warm
# worker ends up speaking a protocol version the dispatcher no longer does.
ARG BLASTBOX_SRC=blastbox
COPY ${BLASTBOX_SRC}/deploy/gvisor/run_warm.py /opt/blastbox/run_warm.py
COPY ${BLASTBOX_SRC}/deploy/firecracker/engines.py /opt/blastbox/engines.py
RUN printf '%s' "${ENGINE}" > /opt/blastbox/engine \
    && mkdir -p /in /out /ctrl \
    && chown 10001:10001 /out /ctrl \
    && find / -xdev -type f -perm /6000 -exec chmod a-s {} + 2>/dev/null || true

USER 10001:10001

# BLASTBOX_GVISOR_WARM_ARGV='["python3","/opt/blastbox/run_warm.py"]'

LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="boxjs-warm-gvisor" \
      org.opencontainers.image.description="box-js warm gVisor C/R rootfs (runsc checkpoint/restore; node + module graph resident)" \
      org.opencontainers.image.version="0.1.0"
